# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx disk file size [name:size[:efi] | name:*[:efi] ...]
        [--man root] [--encrypt] [--device] [--blank]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx disk` makes <file> anew, a disk image of <size> bytes, with a GUID partition table holding the partitions named, in order: what sd(3) serves a disk's partitions from.
A size is a number of bytes, or with `K`, `M` or `G` (binary), and a whole number of 512-byte sectors; the last partition may take `*`, the rest of the disk.
Each partition starts on a 1 MiB boundary, the first at sector 2048, and is typed as Taisce's, or with `:efi` as an EFI system partition, which is left for a FAT formatter; its name is at most 36 characters.
The table is written as UEFI has it: a protective MBR, the primary header and its 128 entries, and their backup at the disk's end, with their checksums; the disk's and each partition's GUID are random.
Each Taisce partition then holds a new Taisce volume, labelled with its name, as fs(4) mounts it; with `--blank`, none is made, for fs(4)'s `--format` to make.
With `--encrypt`, each volume is encrypted (taisce(4)), with a volume key of its own and one slot, opened by the passphrase on the first line of standard input; fs(4) mounts it once `unlock` gives that passphrase.
With `--man`, the first holds the manual of the tree at <root>, its pages at `lib/man/`<section>`/`<name> and the index at `lib/man/index`, as bootfs holds them; the console's shell and cpu(8)'s sessions bind it over bootfs's at `/lib/man`.
The rest of the file is left a hole, so a new image takes little space.

With `--device`, <file> is a disk, such as a microSD card in the Mac's reader (`/dev/rdisk<n>`, which takes `sudo`), and nothing else: its table is rewritten in place, <size> must be at most the disk's, and each partition's first and last MiB are zeroed, so what the disk held before isn't taken for a file system.
Without it, a disk is refused, and with it, a file.
The Orange Pi RV2's card is made so: an EFI system partition for U-Boot and the loader, formatted FAT32 by the Mac's `newfs_msdos`, and the rest a Taisce partition the board formats.

Every boot qemu.sh makes has a disk, made with `vx disk FILE 256M taisce:* --man REPO` if the file isn't there: in a test, anew each run, unless `--disk` names it.

# EXAMPLES

```text
% vx disk build/disk.img 1G esp:64M taisce:*
% echo 'open sesame' | vx disk build/secret.img 256M taisce:* --encrypt
% sudo vx disk /dev/rdisk4 31719424K ESP:1G:efi 'taisce:*' --device --blank
% sudo newfs_msdos -F 32 -v VECTRAOS /dev/rdisk4s1
```

